okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about the laws of logic and conditional  statements so let's go over to here and  talk about you know what are conditional  statements and how can we use them so  first of all what a conditional  statement is it's an if then statement  so  if the hypothesis  then the conclusion so whatever comes  after the if that's your hypothesis  whatever comes after then that's your  conclusion and oftentimes what i'll do  is i'll underline those quantities so  that i know you know which is the  hypothesis and which is a conclusion so  that's called a conditional statement  and this is just an abbreviation with  this arrow that says if p  then q and the thing that we want to pay  attention to is the direction of the  arrow so the arrow is pointing to the  right and what that means is that p if  that happens then q will happen okay so  that's the conditional statement now the  converse statement is said in reverse  you'll notice that we switch the p and  the q if q then p and the thing about  the converse statement is it's not  necessarily true the conditional  statement might be a true statement but  the converse is not necessarily true you  have to check it sometimes it is  sometimes it's not  now the inverse you can see that you're  keeping the p and the q the hypothesis  and the conclusion in the same order but  this little symbol here represents is  the negation so if not p  then not q and again the inverse  is not necessarily true you have to just  check it and then the last one is the  contrapositive and that says if not q  then not p and what we did is we  switched the hypothesis and the  conclusion and we negated both of those  now the thing that you want to remember  is that the conditional statement  and the contrapositive they have the  same truth value means that they're  either both going to be true or they're  both going to be false so if we know  that this conditional statement's true  we automatically know that the  contrapositive is also true and it's the  same thing for the converse and the  inverse if this is true this will be  true if this is false you know that'll  be false so there are called equivalent  statements now as far as the law of  logic goes  we've got the law of detachment and the  law of syllogism you don't need to know  those names but you just need to know  how they work and that's that you know  if p happens then q will happen so if p  occurs we know that q is going to occur  the most common thing that sometimes  comes up in these problems they try to  trick you a little bit is they'll say  q happened does that mean that p  happened  not necessarily it remember it has to go  in the direction of the arrow p causes q  not the other way around right and then  the last one here is the law of  syllogism and what the law of syllogism  is again you don't have to know this  name but  it's basically that the transitive  property if p causes q  and q causes r  then if p happens through this chain of  events r will happen so that's called  the law of syllogism these are basically  the laws of logic again you just want to  pay attention to the direction of the  arrow you know what causes what not not  necessarily in reverse right so let's  see if we can look at a quick example  here if you study your math then you  will get a good grade let's just assume  that that's a true statement okay we'll  just accept that as being true so if  they said  okay john got a good grade  does that mean that he studied his math  not necessarily you can see that's  saying it in reverse that's the converse  and it's not necessarily true  say for example they said  if you don't study your math  then you won't get a good grade now  that's the  inverse we negated those but it's not  necessarily true  just because that conditional that  original statement is true and then the  last one the contrapositive  if john did not get a good grade then he  did not study his math  it might seem a little bit you know  confusing but ironically this is  actually a true statement so when you  switch the hypothesis and the  conclusions we switch those two  statements and you negate them you make  them the opposite that's a true  statement so  assuming that the original statement is  true  if you switch the hypothesis and  conclusion and negate them both that's  the contrapositive it will also be true  just like the conditional so let's get  into an example and we'll see how we can  apply this  so let's just consider that these three  statements below here are true if john  is voted mvp then he'll get a  scholarship so i just like to underline  whatever after the if so if john's voted  mvp  then he'll get a scholarship okay if  john studies engineering he will go to a  local college okay so if he studies  engineering  then he will go to a local college all  right and then the last one  it says that john is not given a  scholarship okay so these are all  considered to be true so what can we  conclude from that so which is  necessarily true  is it true that john did not go to  college  well  let's see we know that he wasn't given a  scholarship but that doesn't necessarily  mean that he didn't go to college  there's nothing that you know gives us  that impression  john was not voted mvp  well let's take a look at that one so  you can see that it says  john is not given a scholarship that's  the negation of this conclusion right  here so if he's not given a scholarship  then it means that he was not voted mvp  that's the contra positive so that's  actually going to be true so letter b is  the correct answer but let's just check  c d for the best answer john went to a  local college well  if they told us that john studied  engineering then we would know that he  went to a local college but otherwise we  really can't make the conclusion he  might have he might not have you know we  don't have a way of proving that and  then the last one john studied  engineering  well he might have but it says if he  studies engineering it doesn't  necessarily said that you know that he  was going to study engineering so the  best answer here is actually letter b  and again it comes back to understanding  the conditional and the contrapositive  for this example  when you switch those and you negate  them that gives you the contrapositive  and it's going to be the same truth  value as the conditional statement so i  hope this helps you understand how to  work with the conditional statements and  the laws of logic this is something you  want to understand for the act there's  not a lot of questions on there about  this maybe just one but again you just  want to have a good understanding of how  this works review this problem to  understand it a little bit better and  i'll see in the next lesson

